`;;;…;;;` is the block form: the payload is a sub-document, so its block
structure survives and the field becomes a fragment of block nodes. A slot or
spec value is **one line** (§6.7), so the block payload writes its breaks as
`\n` — they are materialised before the payload is parsed:

@[Card](/docs "Docs")@@anchorA{body: ;;;## Payload heading\n\nA paragraph with a [[Wiki]] link.\n\n- one\n- two\n\n> quoted line\n;;;}

The inline form drops the payload's **document** wrapper, so a single paragraph
contributes its inline nodes; the block form keeps it, so the same paragraph
arrives inside a `<p>`. That is the only difference between the two spellings —
both run the page's chain, both carry nodes:

@[Card](/docs)@@anchorA{note: ;;still *inline*: no wrapper;;, body: ;;;one paragraph, wrapper kept;;;}

A payload is not a numbering island either: a citation inside it belongs to the
page, and one *before* the body text keeps the earlier number (§6.7, D-RF-8) —
fixture 53 measures that from the other side.
